---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About | Joel Rhine"
  description="Joel Rhine is a software engineer and product builder with a background in coffee, operations, and field leadership."
>
  <header class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">About Joel</p><h1>
      I came to software through the work it is meant to support.
    </h1>
  </header>
  <article class="prose">
    <p>
      Before I wrote software professionally, I worked in coffee, wholesale,
      logistics, and field leadership. Those jobs made the stakes of a bad
      process obvious. When a tool is confusing, the cost shows up as lost time,
      a missed handoff, an unhappy customer, or a person doing extra work to
      cover for the system.
    </p>
    <p>
      I moved into software in 2018 and joined Honey in 2020. From there, I
      worked on merchant integrations, scraping configuration, operational
      dashboards, and on-call systems. At ZeroClick.ai, I worked on automations,
      retailer integrations, experiments, and internal engineering workflows.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today, I am building measure.coffee as its Founder & Founding Engineer. It
      connects my coffee background with the software questions I care about
      most: how people learn, how they make decisions, and how a product can
      help without getting in the way.
    </p>
    <h2>What I bring to a team</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>An operator's attention to the actual workflow</li><li>
        Engineering experience across integrations, automation, and product
        systems
      </li><li>
        A practical approach to communication, collaboration, and handoff
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button button-primary" href="/files/resume.pdf"
        >Download resume</a
      >
    </div>
  </article>
  <section class="section">
    <p class="eyebrow">How I work</p>
    <div class="principles">
      <article>
        <h3>Start with the work itself</h3><p>
          Look for repeated steps, normalized workarounds, and recurring
          questions.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Make complexity usable</h3><p>
          A sophisticated system should not make its users need a manual.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Build for the next person</h3><p>
          Good work is easier to maintain, explain, and extend.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
